 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Popping Artifacts:** Using fewer Gaussians results in larger Gaussians on average, which increases the prevalence of noticeable popping artifacts during camera movement [S4].
-*   **SH Redundancy:** View-dependent colors (SH coefficients) provide minimal benefit during the densification phase; enabling them early wastes resources without improving spatial distribution [S2].
+*   **Popping Artifacts:** Using fewer Gaussians results in larger Gaussians on average, which increases the prevalence of noticeable popping artifacts [S5].
+*   **SH Redundancy:** Incorporating view-dependent colors during densification barely enhances quality but consumes resources; thus, they are disabled until after simplification [S2].
+*   **Depth Estimation:** Depth reinitialization relies on initial training to estimate depth, requiring a few iterations to obtain accurate information [S4].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
-### [Model Compression Pipeline for 3D Gaussian Splatting](9987-model-compression-pipeline-for-3d-gaussian-splatting.md)
-This method is a compression and rendering pipeline for 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) that reduces memory footprint and increases rendering speed while maintaining image quality. It addresses the high memory bandwidth and computational complexity of standard 3DGS, making it suitable for edge deployment, mobile VR/AR, and low-power hardware. It sets itself apart from its parent [Mini-Splatting](7850-mini-splatting.md) by not imposing a hard limit on the total number of Gaussians via mixed-integer optimization, but rather by iteratively pruning, quantizing attributes, and reducing spherical harmonic (SH) degrees to achieve high compression ratios (up to 51.6×) with minimal quality loss. It differs from LightGaussian by using iterative pruning with intermediate fine-tuning, progressive SH-degree reduction, and vector quantization (VQ) on all SH coefficients and colors, rather than only low-salience subsets.
-
 ### Mini-Splatting-D
-A quality-prioritized variant of Mini-Splatting that omits the simplification process to maximize rendering quality [S2]. On Mip-NeRF360 (Outdoor), Mini-Splatting-D uses 4.69M Gaussians, achieving SSIM 0.831, PSNR 27.51, LPIPS 0.176 [S2]. It surpasses Zip-NeRF in SSIM (0.828) and LPIPS (0.189) on this dataset [S2]. Training time is 31m48s, Peak GPU Memory is 7.45GB, Rendering FPS is 83, Peak Rendering Memory is 3.12GB [S2].
+A quality-prioritized variant of Mini-Splatting that omits the simplification process to maximize rendering quality [S2]. It retains the densification phase (Blur Split and Depth Reinitialization) but skips the Intersection Preserving and Sampling steps. This results in a higher Gaussian count (~4.69M vs ~0.49M for standard Mini-Splatting) but achieves superior SSIM and LPIPS metrics, surpassing Zip-NeRF on Mip-NeRF360 Outdoor [S2].
 
 ### Mini-Splatting-C
-Designed for scenarios requiring storage compression, this pipeline is directly based on Mini-Splatting [S2]. Given a pre-trained Mini-Splatting model, it stores Gaussian centers as float32, while other attributes (scales, SH coefficients) are processed using transform coding and standard zip compression [S2].
+A storage-compression variant designed for scenarios requiring reduced file size. It is based on the Mini-Splatting pipeline but applies transform coding and standard zip compression to attributes (scales, SH coefficients) after training, storing only Gaussian centers as float32 [S2].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-**Time and Memory Complexity:**
-*   **Training Time:** Mini-Splatting reduces training time significantly, taking 17m56s compared to 30m8s for vanilla 3DGS on Mip-NeRF360 (Outdoor) [S2].
-*   **Peak GPU Memory:** Reduced from 7.45GB (3DGS) to 2.61GB (Mini-Splatting) on Mip-NeRF360 (Outdoor) [S2].
-*   **Rendering FPS:** Increases from 98 FPS (3DGS) to 410 FPS (Mini-Splatting) on Mip-NeRF360 (Outdoor) [S2].
-*   **Peak Rendering Memory:** Reduced from 2.79GB (3DGS) to 0.40GB (Mini-Splatting) [S2].
-
-**Measured Performance:**
-*   **Mip-NeRF360 (Outdoor):** Mini-Splatting uses 0.49M Gaussians (7x fewer than 3DGS's 3.36M) [S2]. Metrics: SSIM 0.822 (vs 0.815), PSNR 27.34 (vs 27.21), LPIPS 0.217 (vs 0.214) [S2].
-*   **Mip-NeRF360 (General):** Another variant achieves SSIM 0.763, PSNR 25.83, LPIPS 0.273, with a model size of 18.2 MB [S3].
-*   **Model Size:** Up to 10x reduction compared to original 3DGS [S4].
-
-**Strengths and Limitations:**
-*   **Strengths:** Efficient spatial distribution reduces redundancy; significant memory and speed improvements; comparable or better visual quality than dense 3DGS [S2].
-*   **Limitations:** Fewer Gaussians lead to larger individual splats, increasing popping artifacts [S4]. The hard constraint requires careful balancing of densification and simplification schedules [S2].
-
-**Applicability:**
-Mini-Splatting is suitable for resource-constrained environments, such as mobile AR/VR and edge devices, where memory bandwidth, training time, or rendering FPS are critical constraints [S1, unofficial][S2]. Mini-Splatting-D is preferred when quality is prioritized over memory/speed constraints [S2].
+*   **Memory Footprint:** Significantly reduced. On Mip-NeRF360 (Outdoor), Mini-Splatting uses 0.49M Gaussians compared to 3.36M for vanilla 3DGS, a ~7x reduction [S2]. Peak GPU Memory during training is 2.61GB vs 7.45GB for 3DGS [S2]. Peak Rendering Memory is 0.40GB vs 2.79GB [S2].
+*   **Training Time:** Faster. 17m56s vs 30m8s for 3DGS on Mip-NeRF360 (Outdoor) [S2]. Mini-Splatting-D takes 31m48s [S2].
+*   **Rendering Speed:** Higher FPS. 410 FPS vs 98 FPS for 3DGS on Mip-NeRF360 (Outdoor) [S2]. Mini-Splatting-D achieves 83 FPS [S2].
+*   **Visual Quality:** Comparable or superior. On Mip-NeRF360 (Outdoor), Mini-Splatting achieves SSIM 0.822 (vs 0.815), PSNR 27.34 (vs 27.21), LPIPS 0.217 (vs 0.214) [S2]. Mini-Splatting-D achieves SSIM 0.831, PSNR 27.51, LPIPS 0.176 [S2]. On MipNeRF360 generally, Mini-Splatting achieves SSIM 0.763, PSNR 25.83, LPIPS 0.273, with a model size of 18.2 MB [S3].
+*   **Scaling:** The method scales well to resource-constrained environments, enabling training on consumer-grade GPUs (e.g., GTX 1060 6G) [S2].
+*   **Limitations:** The hard constraint $N_{max}$ may lead to larger average Gaussian sizes, increasing popping artifacts [S5]. The method requires careful tuning of the densification/simplification schedule [S2].
 
 ## Background
-Mini-Splatting was introduced by Fang and Wang in 2024 [S2]. It emerged from the observation that vanilla 3DGS suffers from inefficient spatial distribution, leading to overlapping and under-reconstruction [S2]. The method formulates the optimization as a mixed-integer problem to bound memory footprints explicitly [S1, unofficial]. It is part of the broader effort to make 3DGS viable for resource-constrained environments, such as mobile AR/VR and edge devices [S1, unofficial][S2].
+Mini-Splatting was introduced by Guangchi Fang and Bing Wang from the Spatial Intelligence Group at The Hong Kong Polytechnic University [S2]. It was published in 2024 as part of the ECCV proceedings [S2]. The method addresses the "overlapping" and "under-reconstruction" phenomena observed in vanilla 3DGS, where Gaussians are not uniformly distributed around object surfaces [S2]. It builds on the principles of 3D Gaussian Splatting [17] but introduces a mixed-integer optimization formulation to explicitly bound the Gaussian count [S1, unofficial].
 
